   {
    "id": "q17",
    "qtype": "long_answer",
    "marks": 5,
    "text": "Prove that $\\dfrac{\\tan\\theta}{1-\\cot\\theta}+\\dfrac{\\cot\\theta}{1-\\tan\\theta}=1+\\sec\\theta\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta$.",
    "answer": "Proved",
    "solution": "$\\frac{\\tan\\theta}{1-\\cot\\theta}=\\frac{\\sin\\theta/\\cos\\theta}{(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)/\\sin\\theta}=\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$. Similarly $\\frac{\\cot\\theta}{1-\\tan\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)}=-\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$. Adding, LHS $=\\frac{\\sin^3\\theta-\\cos^3\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}=\\frac{(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)(\\sin^2\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}=\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}+1=1+\\sec\\theta\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta=$ RHS.",
    "marking_scheme": [
     {
      "step": "Converting tan and cot into sin and cos",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Simplifying the first term to $\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Simplifying the second term to $-\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$ and combining over a common denominator to get $\\frac{\\sin^3\\theta-\\cos^3\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Factorising $a^3-b^3$ and cancelling $(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Using $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1$ and splitting to get $1+\\sec\\theta\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta$",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ],
